  "reason": "Complete construction and prerequisite audit; see research/phase-2-next-20-batch-4.notes.md and coverage.json. Source D, Example 4.2.2 p.218 and §4.5 opening p.240; documented alternative-proof disposition, no raw fetch claim. Grouping independence and conditioning give E[Y_{n+1}|F_n]=0, so S is a martingale. Each S_n is L2: repeated (a+b)^2<=2a^2+2b^2 suffices, or the finite square expansion with Cauchy–Schwarz. The square Y_k^2 is measurable relative to sigma(Y_k), independent of F_{k-1}, and integrable; its conditional expectation is the constant sigma_k^2 by the independent-variable lemma. Substitute into the bracket definition and apply the square-minus-bracket theorem. To distinguish the two brackets, take just Y_1 distributed as -1,0,1 with probabilities 1/4,1/2,1/4 and Y_k=0 for k>=2. Then [S]_1=Y_1^2 varies while <S>_1=1/2. This finite witness uses no B item as a supplier. AC is inherited from the Radon–Nikodym construction of conditional expectation; if a sequence of versions is needed, AC selects measurable versions at the countably many times. Identities remain identities of almost-sure classes, without completing the filtration.",
